4</mn><msup><mi>Cl</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>aq</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>65</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "9ced38", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996b3"}, {"id": "93ed01", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996b4"}, {"id": "49c899", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996b5"}, {"id": "002aae", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996b6"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996b7", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0df91"], "topics": ["60c9e848ea076dc381c0df5b"], "concepts": ["6117b5e4478ae845c43c5e4d"], "question": {"text": "If the conductivity of mercury at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>07</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> and the resistance of a cell containing mercury is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>243</mn><mi>&#937;</mi><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;then the cell constant of the cell is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is&nbsp;______ . (Nearest integer)"}, "options": [{"id": "3f0583", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996b8"}, {"id": "9a2ae2", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996b9"}, {"id": "6f2b93", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996ba"}, {"id": "cec3a1", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996bb"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996bc", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0df91"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4780"], "concepts": ["61489bb3f154cd4590457c66"], "question": {"text": "Consider the following electrochemical cell at standard condition.<br />\\(\\begin{aligned}<br />& \\mathrm{Au}(\\mathrm{s})\\left|\\mathrm{QH}_2, \\mathrm{Q}\\right| \\mathrm{NH}_4 \\mathrm{X}(0.01 \\mathrm{M})| | \\mathrm{Ag}^{+}(1 \\mathrm{M}) \\mid \\mathrm{Ag}(\\mathrm{s}) \\\\<br>& \\mathrm{E}_{\\text {cell }}=+0.4 \\mathrm{~V}<br />\\end{aligned}\\)<br />The couple \\(\\mathrm{QH}_2 / \\mathrm{Q}\\) represents quinhydrone electrode, the half cell reaction is given below<br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fo-CLozdN3jEnUeNZlJ1pEsvN_QRpgugJTyhaLmZDMFk.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/><br />The \\(\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}\\) value of the ammonium halide salt \\(\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_4 \\mathrm{X}\\right)\\) used here is _________. (nearest integer)"}, "options": [{"id": "4ed2ee", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996bd"}, {"id": "cd84cc", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996be"}, {"id": "0a1461", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996bf"}, {"id": "d40024", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996c0"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996c1", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0df91"], "topics": ["60c9e848ea076dc381c0df5b"], "concepts": ["60e567fb499d1e49588fa02f"], "question": {"text": "Resistance of a conductivity cell (cell constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>129</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>) filled with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>74</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>ppm</mi></math> solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KCl</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> (labelled as solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>). When the same cell is filled with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KCl</mi></math> solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>149</mn><mi>ppm</mi></math>, the resistance is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> (labelled as solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>). The ratio of molar conductivity of solution&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> and solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;is i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mo>&#8743;</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mo>&#8743;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math>&nbsp;is____Given, molar mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KCl</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>74</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mol</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>)"}, "options": [{"id": "ab4772", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996c2"}, {"id": "f44706", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996c3"}, {"id": "ad9a62", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996c4"}, {"id": "e1edcb", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996c5"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996c6", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0df91"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4780"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b69"], "question": {"text": "Electrolysis of 600 mL aqueous solution of NaCl for 5 min changes the pH of the solution to 12 . The current in Amperes used for the given electrolysis is ____. (Nearest integer)."}, "options": [{"id": "d7c1eb", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996c7"}, {"id": "11fb29", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996c8"}, {"id": "404023", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996c9"}, {"id": "66b5b7", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996ca"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996cb", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0df91"], "topics": ["60c9e848ea076dc381c0df5b"], "concepts": ["6117b5e4478ae845c43c5e4d"], "question": {"text": "A <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KCl</mi></math> solution of conductivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> shows a resistance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>19</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> in a conductivity cell. If the same cell is filled with an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HCl</mi></math>&nbsp;solution, the resistance drops to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>03</mn><mi>&#937;</mi></math>. The conductivity of the HCl solution is ____<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. (Round off to the Nearest Integer)."}, "options": [{"id": "358b56", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996cc"}, {"id": "febccd", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996cd"}, {"id": "0c6c2e", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996ce"}, {"id": "396f9a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996cf"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996d0", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0df91"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4780"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4736"], "question": {"text": "<p>An oxidation-reduction reaction in which 3 electrons are transferred has a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>&#916;G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>17</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>37</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kJ</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>25</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mi>cell</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msubsup></math>&nbsp;(in V) is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>_</mo><mo>_</mo><mo>_</mo><mo>_</mo><mo>_</mo><mo>_</mo><mo>_</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>96500</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "d315f3", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996d1"}, {"id": "2ac6b6", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996d2"}, {"id": "86679a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996d3"}, {"id": "0f0cdc", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996d4"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996d5", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0df91"], "topics": ["60c9e848ea076dc381c0df5b"], "concepts": ["60e567fb499d1e49588fa02f"], "question": {"text": "The resistivity of a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math>&nbsp;solution of an electrolyte is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mi>&#937;cm</mi></math>. Its molar conductivity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>cm</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. (Nearest integer)"}, "options": [{"id": "4a8993", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996d6"}, {"id": "4e6eb8", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996d7"}, {"id": "f9d6cf", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996d8"}, {"id": "34699a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8a2ee0e063d92996d9"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283c623cf4b3ad8f125e5", "title": "JEE Numerical", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T